    "reason": "While lowering precision generally reduces storage size, the assertion uses 'directly' as a sufficient condition without accounting for overhead. A concrete counterexample is quantization-aware training or specific sparse quantization schemes where metadata (e.g., scale factors, zero-point offsets, sparsity masks) can increase total storage size despite lower bit-widths for weights/activations. In",
    "counterexample": "A model using 4-bit weights with a large overhead of per-channel scale factors and zero-points in a specific quantization format (e.g., QLinearInt8 with many channels) might result in a larger total file size than the same model stored in float16 if the metadata overhead exceeds the savings from bit-width reduction.",
